# Transport Modes

Select the transport protocol based on your network environment. UDP mode offers performance, while TCP mode provides compatibility and stealth.

## UDP Mode (QUIC)

{% card title="UDP Mode (QUIC)" icon="wifi" description="Uses QUIC protocol over UDP for high-performance tunneling with built-in encryption and multiplexing." %}

`--udp` or append `/udp` to address (default)

**Performance**

| Streams | Throughput |
|---------|------------|
| 1 | 3.5 Gbps |
| 2 | 3.1 Gbps |
| 3 | 2.9 Gbps |
| 4 | 2.8 Gbps |
| 5 | 2.7 Gbps |

**Use Cases**

- Direct connections with minimal overhead
- High-throughput network operations
- Environments allowing UDP traffic
- Performance-critical scenarios

**Characteristics**

- Lower latency than TCP
- Built-in TLS 1.3 encryption
- Connection migration support
- 0-RTT connection resumption
- Efficient multiplexing

{% callout type="note" %}
**Limitations:** May be blocked by strict firewalls. Not suitable for environments requiring proxy traversal.
{% /callout %}

{% /card %}

## TCP Mode (WebSockets)

{% card title="TCP Mode (WebSockets)" icon="plug" description="Uses WebSockets over TCP for maximum compatibility and proxy traversal capabilities." %}

`--tcp` or append `/tcp` to address

**Performance**

| Streams | Throughput |
|---------|------------|
| 1 | 4.4 Gbps |
| 2 | 4.5 Gbps |
| 3 | 4.4 Gbps |
| 4 | 4.3 Gbps |

**Use Cases**

- HTTP proxy environments
- Corporate networks with restrictive firewalls
- CDN fronting scenarios
- When UDP is blocked or unavailable

**Characteristics**

- HTTP/HTTPS compatible
- Traverses HTTP proxies
- Works with CDN fronting
- TLS encryption support
- Universal firewall compatibility

**Advantages**

- Can tunnel through corporate proxies
- Appears as legitimate HTTPS traffic
- Compatible with CDN services (potential evasion)

{% /card %}

## Examples

UDP mode for maximum performance:

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443/udp" title="Entry node using QUIC" /%}

TCP mode for proxy traversal:

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443/tcp" title="Entry node using WebSockets" /%}

TCP mode with TLS for CDN fronting:

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443/tcp --tls" title="HTTPS mode for stealth" /%}

{% callout type="warning" title="Default Behavior" %}
If no transport mode is specified, wallhack defaults to UDP mode (QUIC) for optimal performance. Use `--tcp` when operating through restrictive networks or when stealth is a priority.
{% /callout %}

{% callout type="info" title="CDN Fronting with TCP Mode" %}
When using TCP mode with TLS, you can potentially front your traffic through CDN services. This makes the tunnel appear as legitimate HTTPS traffic to a popular CDN domain, providing an additional layer of obfuscation and making detection more difficult.
{% /callout %}
